**Read:** [International Chronostratigraphic Chart — ICS and IUGS](../sources/International-Chronostratigraphic-Chart.lingua#r_6RWJCRCKJD7BZS5RRKVKGE8MT3), legend, hierarchy, edition notes, and selected boundary information; [International Stratigraphic Guide, abridged version — ICS](../sources/International-Stratigraphic-Guide.lingua#r_6NAYJE2KFXGRNCWP6TEY7AZE7J), stratotypes, chronostratigraphy, and unit relationships in full; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the complete geological-time material.

A geological timescale connects named units, boundary definitions, correlations, and numerical-age information. Distinguish time units from the corresponding rock units and a boundary definition from its current numerical calibration. A later age revision need not mean that the defining stratigraphic relationship has been replaced.

Read the chart's edition, hierarchy, symbols, notes, and uncertainty conventions. Use the official stratigraphic guide to understand stratotypes and the evidence defining different unit types. A printed color chart is a compact synthesis; it does not supply every measurement, correlation, or interpretive argument supporting it.

Translations and dynamic/static editions can lag one another. The consulted ICS supplementary page identifies a 2026/06 English static chart and a 2024/12 Brazilian Portuguese translation while retaining some older update text. Cite the actual version used in a comparison and check the underlying definitions rather than silently combining boundary numbers from different editions.

**Work:** locate a selected interval and boundary in an identified chart, trace its definition and numerical-age evidence through authoritative references, and explain what would change under a new calibration. Reconcile one historical textbook date with a later official edition without treating all old geological reasoning as invalid.

Read the full chart notes and guide, then use them throughout the Earth-history sequence. Precise terminology supports communication across field geology, paleontology, geochemistry, and planetary science without replacing the evidence-specific work of each.

**Continue:** [Infer Earth’s interior from several kinds of evidence](../earth/Interior-structure-and-indirect-evidence.lingua#r_2YEXGGAP9CA97F2N19K6CKXKF6).

